<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>RAG Lab — Interactive Simulator</title><style>
:root{--bg:#f5f5f7;--card:#fff;--text:#171717;--muted:#737373;--line:#e5e5e7;--blue:#0071e3;--blue2:#eaf4ff;--green:#18864b;--dark:#111820;--shadow:0 18px 55px #00000014;--r:24px}*{box-sizing:border-box}html{scroll-behavior:smooth}body{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--text);font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"SF Pro Display","Segoe UI",Arial,sans-serif;-webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased}.top{height:68px;position:sticky;top:0;z-index:5;background:#f5f5f7cc;backdrop-filter:blur(22px);border-bottom:1px solid #0000000a}.topin{max-width:1240px;height:100%;margin:auto;padding:0 22px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between}.logo{font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.03em}.logo b{color:var(--blue)}.badge{font-size:12px;color:#666;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);padding:7px 11px;border-radius:99px}.layout{max-width:1240px;margin:auto;padding:34px 22px 70px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:260px 1fr;gap:24px}.side{position:sticky;top:92px;height:max-content}.side h4{font-size:12px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.09em;color:#8a8a8f;margin:0 0 12px 12px}.steps{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px}.step{border:0;background:transparent;text-align:left;padding:13px 12px;border-radius:14px;color:#666;display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:center}.step:hover{background:#e9e9eb}.step.active{background:#fff;color:#111;box-shadow:0 6px 22px #0000000f}.num{width:27px;height:27px;border-radius:50%;background:#e8e8ea;display:grid;place-items:center;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;flex:none}.step.active .num{background:var(--blue);color:#fff}.progress{height:5px;background:#e1e1e3;border-radius:9px;margin:20px 12px;overflow:hidden}.progress i{display:block;height:100%;background:var(--blue);width:14.28%;transition:.35s}.note{font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:#888;padding:15px 12px}.hero{padding:22px 4px 32px}.eyebrow{color:var(--blue);font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:12px}.hero h1{font-size:clamp(42px,6vw,76px);line-height:.96;letter-spacing:-.07em;margin:0 0 18px}.hero p{font-size:18px;color:#666;max-width:700px;margin:0;line-height:1.5}.stage{display:none}.stage.active{display:block;animation:in .35s ease}@keyframes in{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(8px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}.panel{background:#ffffffd1;border:1px solid #0000000a;box-shadow:var(--shadow);border-radius:var(--r);padding:26px;margin-bottom:20px}.head{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;align-items:flex-start;margin-bottom:22px}.head h2{margin:0;font-size:27px;letter-spacing:-.045em}.head p{margin:6px 0 0;color:var(--muted);font-size:14px}textarea{width:100%;min-height:125px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:17px;background:#fafafa;padding:16px;resize:vertical;outline:none}textarea:focus,input:focus{border-color:#9bcaff;box-shadow:0 0 0 4px var(--blue2)}button{font:inherit;cursor:pointer}.primary{border:0;background:#111;color:#fff;border-radius:999px;padding:11px 17px;font-weight:650}.secondary{border:1px solid var(--line);background:#fff;color:#222;border-radius:999px;padding:10px 16px;font-weight:600}.actions{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:15px}.docgrid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px}.doc,.chunk,.metric{border:1px solid var(--line);background:#fff;border-radius:18px;padding:17px}.doc h3{margin:0 0 7px;font-size:16px}.doc p{margin:0;color:#777;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45}.doc small{display:inline-block;margin-top:12px;color:#999}.chunk{background:#fafafa;padding:13px;margin:9px 0}.chunktop{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-bottom:6px}.chunk mark{background:#fff0c2;border-radius:4px;padding:1px 3px}.score{font-weight:700;color:var(--green)}.query{display:flex;gap:10px}.query input{flex:1;border:1px solid var(--line);background:#fff;border-radius:15px;padding:13px 15px;outline:none}.flow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(5,1fr);gap:10px}.flowcard{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:18px;padding:16px;position:relative}.flowcard:after{content:"→";position:absolute;right:-13px;top:50%;color:#aaa}.flowcard:last-child:after{display:none}.icon{width:35px;height:35px;border-radius:11px;background:var(--blue2);color:var(--blue);display:grid;place-items:center;font-weight:800;margin-bottom:13px}.flowcard h3{font-size:14px;margin:0 0 6px}.flowcard p{font-size:12px;color:#777;margin:0;line-height:1.45}.answer{background:var(--dark);color:#fff;border-radius:20px;padding:22px}.label{font-size:11px;color:#9da7af;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em;margin-bottom:9px}.answer p{margin:0;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55}.citation{margin-top:14px;padding-top:12px;border-top:1px solid #ffffff1f;font-size:12px;color:#aeb6bd}.explain{background:var(--blue2);border:1px solid #d5e9ff;padding:16px;border-radius:17px;color:#24547e;font-size:14px;line-height:1.55;margin-top:15px}.metrics{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:10px;margin-top:15px}.metric strong{display:block;font-size:22px;letter-spacing:-.03em}.metric span{color:#888;font-size:11px}.footnav{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;margin-top:18px}.done{background:#e9f8ef;color:#176b3e;border:1px solid #cfeeda;padding:10px 13px;border-radius:15px;font-size:12px}@media(max-width:900px){.layout{grid-template-columns:1fr}.side{position:static}.steps{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(7,1fr)}.step{justify-content:center;padding:8px}.step span:not(.num),.side h4,.note{display:none}.progress{margin:10px 0 20px}.flow{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}.flowcard:after{display:none}.docgrid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}@media(max-width:560px){.top{height:58px}.topin{padding:0 14px}.badge{display:none}.layout{padding:20px 14px 50px}.panel{padding:18px;border-radius:19px}.metrics{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}.flow{grid-template-columns:1fr}.hero h1{font-size:48px}.hero p{font-size:16px}}
</style></head><body>
<header class="top"><div class="topin"><div class="logo">RAG<b>Lab</b></div><div class="badge">Interactive RAG simulator · one HTML file</div></div></header>
<div class="layout"><aside class="side"><h4>RAG pipeline</h4><div class="steps"><button class="step active" onclick="go(1)"><span class="num">1</span><span>Question</span></button><button class="step" onclick="go(2)"><span class="num">2</span><span>Documents</span></button><button class="step" onclick="go(3)"><span class="num">3</span><span>Chunking</span></button><button class="step" onclick="go(4)"><span class="num">4</span><span>Retrieval</span></button><button class="step" onclick="go(5)"><span class="num">5</span><span>Context</span></button><button class="step" onclick="go(6)"><span class="num">6</span><span>Generation</span></button><button class="step" onclick="go(7)"><span class="num">7</span><span>Result</span></button></div><div class="progress"><i id="bar"></i></div><div class="note">Move through the pipeline to see how a typical Retrieval-Augmented Generation system turns a question into a grounded answer.</div></aside>
<main><section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">LEARN BY MOVING THROUGH THE PIPELINE</div><h1>See how RAG<br>actually works.</h1><p>Ask a question, watch documents become searchable knowledge, retrieve relevant chunks, and see how grounded context reaches the final answer.</p></section>
<section class="stage active" id="s1"><div class="panel"><div class="head"><div><h2>1. Start with a question</h2><p>The user asks something the model needs to answer.</p></div></div><textarea id="question">What is RAG and why is it useful?</textarea><div class="actions"><button class="primary" onclick="next()">Start RAG →</button></div></div></section>
<section class="stage" id="s2"><div class="panel"><div class="head"><div><h2>2. Your knowledge base</h2><p>RAG first needs external information that can be searched.</p></div></div><div class="docgrid"><div class="doc"><h3>rag-guide.md</h3><p>RAG combines retrieval with generation so a model can use relevant external context.</p><small>1,248 tokens</small></div><div class="doc"><h3>architecture.md</h3><p>A typical pipeline contains documents, chunking, embeddings, vector search and generation.</p><small>932 tokens</small></div><div class="doc"><h3>evaluation.md</h3><p>Grounded answers can be evaluated for retrieval quality, faithfulness and relevance.</p><small>756 tokens</small></div></div><div class="explain"><strong>Why this matters:</strong> the model can use information brought in at query time instead of relying only on information encoded in its parameters.</div><div class="footnav"><button class="secondary" onclick="prev()">← Back</button><button class="primary" onclick="next()">Chunk documents →</button></div></div></section>
<section class="stage" id="s3"><div class="panel"><div class="head"><div><h2>3. Chunk the documents</h2><p>Large documents are split into smaller pieces that can be retrieved independently.</p></div></div><div class="chunk"><div class="chunktop"><span>Chunk 01 · rag-guide.md</span><span>~180 tokens</span></div>RAG is a system that combines <mark>retrieval</mark> with <mark>generation</mark> to provide a language model with relevant external context.</div><div class="chunk"><div class="chunktop"><span>Chunk 02 · architecture.md</span><span>~160 tokens</span></div>A RAG pipeline commonly includes document ingestion, <mark>chunking</mark>, embeddings, a vector database and retrieval.</div><div class="chunk"><div class="chunktop"><span>Chunk 03 · evaluation.md</span><span>~140 tokens</span></div>Evaluation can measure whether retrieved passages are <mark>relevant</mark> and whether generated answers are grounded in them.</div><div class="explain"><strong>Think of chunks as index cards.</strong> Instead of searching an entire book every time, the system can locate a few small pieces that contain the information needed for the question.</div><div class="footnav"><button class="secondary" onclick="prev()">← Back</button><button class="primary" onclick="next()">Retrieve relevant chunks →</button></div></div></section>
<section class="stage" id="s4"><div class="panel"><div class="head"><div><h2>4. Retrieve</h2><p>The query is compared against indexed content to find relevant chunks.</p></div></div><div class="query"><input id="query" value="What is RAG and why is it useful?" readonly><button class="primary" onclick="runSearch()">Search</button></div><div id="results"><div class="chunk"><div class="chunktop"><span>Similarity result</span><span class="score">0.94</span></div>RAG combines retrieval with generation so a model can use relevant external context.</div><div class="chunk"><div class="chunktop"><span>Similarity result</span><span class="score">0.88</span></div>A typical pipeline contains documents, chunking, embeddings, vector search and generation.</div><div class="chunk"><div class="chunktop"><span>Similarity result</span><span class="score">0.73</span></div>Grounded answers can be evaluated for retrieval quality, faithfulness and relevance.</div></div><div class="metrics"><div class="metric"><strong>3</strong><span>chunks retrieved</span></div><div class="metric"><strong>0.85</strong><span>avg. relevance</span></div><div class="metric"><strong>Top-K</strong><span>retrieval strategy</span></div><div class="metric"><strong>Vector</strong><span>similarity search</span></div></div><div class="footnav"><button class="secondary" onclick="prev()">← Back</button><button class="primary" onclick="next()">Build context →</button></div></div></section>
<section class="stage" id="s5"><div class="panel"><div class="head"><div><h2>5. Build the context</h2><p>The retrieved chunks are assembled into context for the language model.</p></div></div><div class="answer"><div class="label">Context sent to the model</div><p>RAG combines retrieval with generation to provide relevant external context. A typical pipeline uses chunking, embeddings, vector search and retrieval before generation.</p><div class="citation">Sources: rag-guide.md · architecture.md · evaluation.md</div></div><div class="explain"><strong>This is the key handoff.</strong> Retrieval finds the evidence; context construction packages that evidence so the generator can use it while answering the original question.</div><div class="footnav"><button class="secondary" onclick="prev()">← Back</button><button class="primary" onclick="next()">Generate answer →</button></div></div></section>
<section class="stage" id="s6"><div class="panel"><div class="head"><div><h2>6. Generate</h2><p>The language model receives the question plus retrieved context and generates a response.</p></div></div><div class="flow"><div class="flowcard"><div class="icon">Q</div><h3>User query</h3><p>What is RAG and why is it useful?</p></div><div class="flowcard"><div class="icon">＋</div><h3>Context</h3><p>Relevant retrieved chunks.</p></div><div class="flowcard"><div class="icon">P</div><h3>Prompt</h3><p>Question + instructions + context.</p></div><div class="flowcard"><div class="icon">AI</div><h3>LLM</h3><p>Generates a grounded response.</p></div><div class="flowcard"><div class="icon">✓</div><h3>Answer</h3><p>Useful response with source context.</p></div></div><div class="footnav"><button class="secondary" onclick="prev()">← Back</button><button class="primary" onclick="next()">See final result →</button></div></div></section>
<section class="stage" id="s7"><div class="panel"><div class="head"><div><h2>7. The final answer</h2><p>Now the model answers using the retrieved context.</p></div><div class="done">Pipeline complete ✓</div></div><div class="answer"><div class="label">Generated answer</div><p>RAG, or Retrieval-Augmented Generation, is a pattern where an AI model retrieves relevant information from an external knowledge source and uses that information as context when generating an answer. It is useful because it can ground responses in specific, up-to-date or private information rather than relying only on the model's internal knowledge.</p><div class="citation">Grounded by: rag-guide.md · architecture.md</div></div><div class="explain"><strong>RAG in one line:</strong> Retrieve relevant knowledge → put it into context → generate an answer grounded in that knowledge.</div><div class="actions"><button class="primary" onclick="go(1)">Run it again ↻</button></div></div></section>
</main></div><script>
let current=1;function go(n){current=n;document.querySelectorAll('.stage').forEach(x=>x.classList.remove('active'));document.getElementById('s'+n).classList.add('active');document.querySelectorAll('.step').forEach((x,i)=>x.classList.toggle('active',i===n-1));document.getElementById('bar').style.width=(n/7*100)+'%';window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'});if(n===4){let q=document.getElementById('question').value.trim();if(q)document.getElementById('query').value=q}}function next(){go(Math.min(7,current+1))}function prev(){go(Math.max(1,current-1))}function runSearch(){let b=document.getElementById('results');b.style.opacity='.35';setTimeout(()=>b.style.opacity='1',250)}
</script></body></html>
